<!--BlockML Documentation — a guide to combining inherited members

  FQN: org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs.MemberInheritanceAndOverrides-->
<docs:MemberInheritanceAndOverrides xmlns="http://blockml.org/bml"
  xmlns:docs="org.blockml.bml.bmldocs.a04_rendering.data.page.docs"
  xmlns:dpage="org.blockml.bml.docgen.page"
  xmlns:doc="org.blockml.bml.documentation"
  xmlns:def="org.blockml.bml.definition">

  <baseType>
    dpage:DocPage
  </baseType>
  <is>
    A guide to combining inherited members
  </is>
  <documentation>
    Scope: combining inherited members, the override flag, and refining or finalizing members,
    grounded in org.blockml.bml.documentation.PartialMemberOverrides and
    org.blockml.bml.definition.MemberDefinition. Body content is authored in a03 from those
    sources.
  </documentation>
  <references>
    <see type="doc:PartialMemberOverrides" relation="Requires">
      Primary content source — override modes, partial authoring, and merge rules.
    </see>
    <see type="def:MemberDefinition" relation="Requires">
      Declares abstract, final, override, and readOnly on every member kind.
    </see>
  </references>
  <properties>
    <title override="true">
      <value>Member Inheritance and Overrides</value>
    </title>
    <detailLevel override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.level.Introductory</value>
    </detailLevel>
    <pageType override="true">
      <value>org.blockml.bml.docgen.pagetype.Concept</value>
    </pageType>
  </properties>
  <aggregations>
    <content override="true">
      <composition>
        <dpage:Section heading="Redeclaring an inherited member">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A child Block may redeclare a member it inherits. Whether that redeclaration
introduces a new member or refines the inherited one depends on whether a member of that same
identity already exists above it, in the baseType chain or in a merged contract.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="New member versus override">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>A redeclaration that introduces a member not already present must not mark
itself as an override. A redeclaration that merges with a member already inherited must mark
itself as an override, so the model states plainly that the redefinition is deliberate.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Override is never inherited">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>Each redeclaration layer must set override="true" again on its own — a child
Block never inherits that marker from a parent's redeclaration. Every layer that merges with an
existing member restates the marker for itself.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Partial authoring: only what changes">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>An override needs to restate only the member name, the override marker, and the
attributes it actually changes — every other attribute is inherited unchanged from the parent
declaration.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Finalizing an incomplete member">
          <content>
            <dpage:Paragraph>
              <content>
                <dpage:Text>
                  <text>When a parent Block leaves a member incomplete, a child Block can finalize it by
overriding the member with a complete declaration, supplying whatever the parent's declaration
left open.</text>
                </dpage:Text>
              </content>
            </dpage:Paragraph>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
        <dpage:Section heading="Continue with Visibility">
          <content>
            <dpage:Navigation>
              <title>Next</title>
              <items>
                <dpage:NavigationItem label="Visibility" summary="Learn the naming-convention visibility model for members and Sub-Blocks.">
                  <target>docs:Visibility</target>
                </dpage:NavigationItem>
              </items>
            </dpage:Navigation>
          </content>
        </dpage:Section>
      </composition>
    </content>
  </aggregations>
</docs:MemberInheritanceAndOverrides>
